# Bites Support

A multi-agent, bilingual (Arabic/English) customer support assistant for a food delivery app, built with LangGraph, LangChain, and RAG. It routes customer messages to specialist agents for order tracking, billing/refunds, and policy questions — with a real human-in-the-loop approval flow for high-value refunds.

Built as a focused project to demonstrate the tools and architecture used in modern agentic AI systems: LangGraph, LangChain, tool-calling agents, RAG with a vector store, and multi-agent orchestration.

## What it does

A customer can ask, in Arabic (including Egyptian dialect) or English:
- **"فين الأوردر بتاعي رقم 1001؟"** → tracked via a live order-status tool
- **"my order is 40 minutes late, refund me"** → checked against real policy, then refunded automatically (small amounts) or queued for manager approval (large amounts)
- **"can I combine a promo code with free delivery?"** → answered from a real FAQ knowledge base via RAG, not the model's general knowledge

## Architecture

```
                    ┌──────────────┐
   User message ───▶│  Supervisor  │── classifies intent using recent context
                    └──────┬───────┘
                           │
              ┌────────────┼────────────┐
              ▼            ▼            ▼
        ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────┐
        │  Order   │ │ Billing  │ │   FAQ    │
        │  Agent   │ │  Agent   │ │  Agent   │
        └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘ └────┬─────┘
             │            │            │
     get_order_status  issue_refund  search_faq
     list_customer_    get_order_    (Chroma RAG)
        orders          status
     search_faq       search_faq
```

This is a hand-wired **LangGraph `StateGraph`**, not a single agent with every tool bolted on. The supervisor is a lightweight classification call (not a full tool-calling agent — no tools needed for routing), and each specialist is a separate `create_agent` instance scoped to only the tools relevant to its job. Every specialist also has access to `search_faq`, since policy questions come up naturally inside order and billing conversations, not just in dedicated FAQ threads.

Conversation memory is handled by a **LangGraph SQLite checkpointer**, keyed by a `thread_id` (the chat session ID) — conversations survive a server restart, not just an in-memory dict that resets on every deploy.

## Tech stack

| Piece | Tool | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Agent orchestration | LangGraph + LangChain (`create_agent`) | Matches the target stack directly |
| LLM | Groq (`openai/gpt-oss-120b`) | Free tier, fast inference, swappable via a one-file provider abstraction (`app/llm.py`) — OpenAI, Gemini, or local Ollama models work by changing one env var |
| Vector store | Chroma | Local, free, persistent |
| Embeddings | `intfloat/multilingual-e5-small` (local, via `sentence-transformers`) | Handles Arabic + English without an API call |
| Backend | FastAPI | REST endpoint + static file serving |
| Persistence | SQLite (LangGraph checkpointer + a separate approvals table) | Zero-infra, file-based, swappable for Postgres later |
| Frontend | Vanilla HTML/JS, no framework | Kept dependency-free; session history, session switching, and a live typing indicator, all in one file |

## Design decisions and trade-offs

A few choices are worth calling out explicitly, since they were deliberate rather than defaults:

**Human-in-the-loop via an approval queue, not LangGraph's native `interrupt()`.** Each specialist is invoked as an independent nested `create_agent` call inside its graph node, rather than composed as a true LangGraph subgraph sharing one checkpointed execution tree. `interrupt()` needs the whole run to share that single checkpoint, which this architecture doesn't have. Instead, refunds above 200 EGP write a pending row to a small SQLite `approvals` table; a separate admin page (`/admin.html`) lists and resolves them. This is a legitimate, commonly used pattern in production systems — an action creates a pending record instead of executing immediately, and a human reviews it out of band.

**Tool-call messages are stripped before handing off between specialists.** Different specialists are bound to different tool sets (billing only has `issue_refund`/`get_order_status`/`search_faq`, for example). Passing the *raw* conversation history — including another specialist's tool-call messages — across a handoff caused the LLM provider to reject the request outright, since it validates that every tool call in the message history matches a tool available in that specific call. A `clean_history()` helper strips tool-call scaffolding before each handoff, keeping only human-readable turns shared across specialists.

**Tool docstrings are treated as part of the prompt, not just documentation.** A real bug during development: `get_order_status`'s docstring said "status, items, driver and delay" but never mentioned price — even though the function returned it. The model correctly concluded from the docstring that it couldn't look up prices, and told a customer it didn't have access to data it actually had. Every tool's docstring was audited to describe everything the function actually returns.

**Mock data, not a live database.** A full SQLite schema (customers/orders tables, computed lateness from real timestamps, context-based customer identity instead of an LLM-supplied ID) was built and tested end-to-end during development, then deliberately rolled back in favor of the original in-memory mock data. It didn't change what the demo shows, and added real surface area for something to break. The `OrderRepo` class is still a clean abstraction specifically so this swap is a contained change — only that one class's internals would need to change, not the tools or agents that call it.

**Customer identity, if implemented for real, should never be an LLM-supplied tool argument.** This came up directly while building the (since-reverted) real-DB version: if a tool like "list my orders" takes a customer ID as a parameter the model fills in from conversation text, a confused or manipulated model could pass the wrong ID and leak another customer's data. The correct pattern — validated during that detour, worth keeping in mind for a real implementation — is to derive identity from authenticated session context, never from text the model generates.

**Session identity is a bearer token today, with no authentication layer.** The chat session ID is a UUID stored in the browser's `localStorage`; possessing it is sufficient to resume that conversation. Fine for a demo with no real user data, but a production version would tie sessions to authenticated users and prefer an httpOnly cookie over `localStorage` to resist token theft via XSS.

## Evals

`evals/run_evals.py` runs a fixed set of representative messages through the graph and checks, per case:
1. Did the supervisor route to the correct specialist?
2. Did the expected tool actually fire? (captured live from the existing application logs, not re-instrumented)
3. For the approval-queue case, did a real row land in the approvals database — not just "did the reply contain certain words," which turned out to be a fragile signal since models phrase the same outcome many different ways.

One test case deliberately checks that a refund request *gets denied* when the order doesn't meet the policy threshold — the agent correctly refusing an ineligible refund request is a feature, not a failure, and it's worth having a test that would catch a regression toward blindly approving whatever a customer asks for.

Run with:
```bash
python -m evals.run_evals
```

## Running it locally

```bash
# 1. Set up the environment
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate    # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt   # or the individual packages listed below

# 2. Add your API key
echo "LLM_PROVIDER=groq" >> .env
echo "GROQ_API_KEY=your_key_here" >> .env

# 3. Build the FAQ vector index (first run only, or after editing data/faq/*.md)
python -m app.ingest --force

# 4. Run the server
uvicorn app.main:app

# 5. Open the chat
# http://localhost:8000        -> customer chat
# http://localhost:8000/admin.html -> pending refund approvals
```

**Packages used:** `langgraph`, `langchain`, `langchain-openai`, `langchain-groq`, `langchain-chroma`, `chromadb`, `langchain-huggingface`, `sentence-transformers`, `langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite`, `fastapi`, `uvicorn`, `python-dotenv`.

**Swapping the LLM provider:** change `LLM_PROVIDER` in `.env` to `openai`, `gemini`, or `ollama` — see `app/llm.py`.

## Project structure

```
support-agent/
├── app/
│   ├── graph.py          # the LangGraph StateGraph: supervisor + 3 specialists
│   ├── tools.py          # order/billing tools + mock OrderRepo
│   ├── rag.py            # Chroma retriever + search_faq tool
│   ├── ingest.py         # builds/rebuilds the FAQ vector index
│   ├── approvals.py      # SQLite-backed refund approval queue
│   ├── llm.py            # swappable LLM provider (Groq/OpenAI/Gemini/Ollama)
│   ├── prompts.py        # all system prompts, versioned as config
│   ├── logging_config.py
│   └── main.py           # FastAPI app: /chat, /history, /admin/*
├── data/faq/*.md          # policy content indexed for RAG
├── evals/run_evals.py     # routing + tool-use + side-effect checks
├── static/
│   ├── index.html         # customer chat UI
│   └── admin.html         # refund approval review page
└── checkpoints.sqlite, approvals.db, chroma_db/   # generated, gitignored
```

## What I'd do with more time

- **Real persistence for order data** — a normalized SQLite schema (customers/orders, computed lateness from real timestamps) was already built and tested during development; reintroducing it cleanly, with identity derived from auth rather than conversation text, is the natural next step.
- **LangGraph's native `interrupt()`** for approvals, if the specialists were restructured as true composed subgraphs sharing one checkpoint, instead of the current approval-queue pattern.
- **Authentication and per-user session scoping**, replacing the current bearer-token-style session ID.
- **An Arabic-specialized embedding model** (e.g., research models trained specifically on dialectal Arabic) in place of the general multilingual embedding model currently used — the FAQ content is retrieved correctly today, but a general multilingual model likely underperforms a specialized one on nuanced Egyptian dialect queries specifically.
- **Postgres instead of SQLite** for the checkpointer and approvals store, for real concurrent-write safety at scale.